---
arrangement: why-reason-cmdsub-opener
owns:
  - why-reason-cmdsub-closer
  - why-remediation-resolve-dynamism
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# The operand is a command-substitution, so the analyzer cannot know its value until the host
# runs it -- the site runs, and the why-report's because-line is the whole cause chain.
apt-get install -y "$(cat /etc/webhost/pkgset)"

-- replay --
$ dorc why book.sh:4 --book=book.sh
      [ book.sh, participating lines:
   4 | apt-get install -y "$(cat /etc/webhost/pkgset)"

   [unwritten: why-participating-lines-closure]

   === OUTCOME ===

   4|apt-get ran RATHER THAN guarded:
      ran because operand 3 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value --
      its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 4:1 `apt-get install -y "$(cat
      /etc/webhost/pkgset)"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it,
      make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]

(Receipt analysis: the run's own records, nothing re-measured. Filtered for presumed
relevance; `--all` enumerates everything the engine holds.)
